<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Munster</h1><p class="st-meta">1287 CE – 1679 CE (approx.) · capital Munster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Alsatian imperial city in the Vosges valley of Saint Gregory, grown around an old abbey and remembered today for its cheese.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> grew around the Benedictine abbey of Saint Gregory, founded in the 7th century in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> valley west of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-colmar">Colmar</a>. The town that formed beside the monastery won its freedom from the abbots and was recognised as an imperial city by Rudolf I in 1287. It later joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the league of Alsatian imperial cities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The town shared authority over the valley with the abbey in a long and often bitter relationship. Its people lived from dairy farming, cheese, textiles and the valley's trade. The town and much of the valley adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> in the 1530s, while the abbey remained Catholic. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops occupied the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1679 confirmed French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a> was almost completely destroyed in the fighting of 1914 to 1915. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> famed for Munster cheese.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1287 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.31%;width:7.80%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1287 CE – 1679 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munster</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alsatian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Munster (Alsace), Münster im Gregoriental</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1483&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-munster" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Munster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 496 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Munster lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 752L5918 1245"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5918 1245h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Munster. <a href="/atlas?year=1483&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-munster">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

